[Line "Soltis Variation: 13.Bg5 Rc5 14.Kb1"]
[Section "9.Bc4: Soltis Variation and 12.Kb1"]
[Summary "White can force this system with the 10.h4 move order. Black closes the h-file with ...h5, posts a rook on c5 and meets the g4 and h5 sacrifices by taking everything and returning the exchange."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. h4 {
[%cal Rh4h5] White starts with the h-pawn. The point of the move order is to rule out the Topalov
Variation: with h5 threatened, Black has no time for ...Rc8, ...Nxd4 and ...b5. The price is that White
is committed to h4, so the quiet 12.Kb1 systems are gone as well. }
( 10. Bb3 { The third move order. } 10... Rc8 { Keeping both systems: 11.O-O-O Nxd4 12.Bxd4 b5 is the
Topalov Variation. The independent alternative is 10...Nxd4 11.Bxd4 b5 at once, the original form of
Topalov's idea. } 11. h4 h5 12. O-O-O { The same position as
after 12.Bb3 in the main line. } 12... Ne5 )
10... h5 { [%csl Yh5,Rg5] The Soltis move, and here the only good one: Jones calls it dangerous to let
the pawn go any further. With the pawn on h5 White can open the h-file only by sacrificing with g4. The
cost is the g5-square, which is where White's bishop goes next. } 11. O-O-O Rc8 12. Bb3 Ne5 { [%cal
Ge5c4] Now the knight belongs on e5. With h4 and ...h5 inserted the Topalov plan is less good: after
12...Nxd4 13.Bxd4 b5 White plays g4, and ...h5 has turned from a defence into a target. On e5 the knight
eyes c4 and also covers g4 and f3. } 13. Bg5 { [%cal Rg5f6,Rf3f4,Rc3d5] The main line. The bishop takes
the square ...h5 gave up, pins the knight to e7 and prepares f4 followed by e5, or Bxf6 and Nd5. The
alternatives 13.Bh6, 13.Kb1 and 13.g4 are in a later line. } 13... Rc5 { [%cal Gc5g5,Gb7b5] [%csl Yd5]
The key move of the Soltis, and not one that anybody finds at the board. On c5 the rook covers d5, so
that Nd5 can be answered by ...Rxd5; it frees the b-pawn; and it looks along the rank at the bishop on
g5. As soon as the knight leaves e5 Black can take that bishop. The exchange sacrifice removes White's
best attacking piece, drags a pawn to g5 where it blocks its own files, and leaves Black's knight and
bishop unopposed on the dark squares. } 14. Kb1 { The usual prophylactic move, taking the king off the
c-file before the fight starts. 14.g4 and 14.f4 are in the next line. } 14... b5 { [%cal Gb5b4,Ge5c4]
Jones's recommendation: Black simply gets on with it. }
( 14... Re8 { The older main line. The rook protects e7 so that the queen is free to go to a5 or c8;
Carlsen played it against Radjabov in 2008. It is a sound alternative for a player who wants the rook
on e8 before committing the b-pawn. } )
15. g4 { [%cal Rh4h5] White cannot wait for ...Nc4 or ...b4 and gives a pawn to open the h-file. }
15... hxg4 { Jones prefers taking. After 15...a5, which has also been played, he names 16.gxh5 and
16.Bxf6 as the critical replies. } 16. h5 { A second pawn, to pull the knight away from f6. }
16... Nxh5 17. Nd5 { [%cal Rd5e7,Rg5e7] Now e7 is attacked twice. } 17... Re8 { The calm defence and the
most popular move. 17...Rxd5 at once has also been played. } 18. Rxh5 { The standard blow: the rook is
given to reopen the file that the knight was blocking. } 18... gxh5 19. Qh2 { [%cal Rh2h5] Heading for
h5, with threats against h7 and f7. } 19... Rxd5 { [%cal Gd8b6] Black returns the exchange to remove the
knight, which was tying the queen and the rook to e7. } 20. Bxd5 Qb6 { **Verdict:** critical and
double-edged. Black is two pawns up for the moment. White will win one back on h5 and has the h-file;
Black has the knight on e5, pressure on d4 and b2, and ...e6 to drive the bishop from d5.

This is where Jones's recommended moves lead in practice. It is sharper than anything in the Topalov
lines and has to be prepared move by move: here the plans alone are not enough. } *

[Line "Soltis Variation: 13.Bg5 Rc5 with 14.g4 and 14.f4"]
[Section "9.Bc4: Soltis Variation and 12.Kb1"]
[Summary "White breaks at once without Kb1. Black takes the pawn, hits the queen with ...Nc4 and has the bishop on g5 under fire from the rook."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. h4 h5
11. O-O-O Rc8 12. Bb3 Ne5 13. Bg5 Rc5 14. g4 { White sacrifices at once, saving the king move. }
( 14. f4 { [%cal Rf4f5,Re4e5] Chasing the knight to prepare e5. The drawback is that f3 no longer covers
e4 and g4, and the bishop on g5 has lost its way back to e3. } 14... Nc4 15. Qd3 Na5 { [%cal Ga5b3] The
knight goes after the bishop on b3, the piece that makes e5 and f5 dangerous. This is the move elite
players have chosen most often, with good results in a small number of games; 15...b5 16.e5 is the
sharper alternative. } )
14... hxg4 15. f4 { [%cal Rf4f5,Re4e5] White wants to drive the knight off and follow with f5 or e5. }
( 15. h5 Nxh5 { Black is two pawns up and the knight blocks the h-file. It is the main line of the
previous game without Kb1 and ...b5, and White has to prove his compensation without the useful king
move. } )
15... Nc4 { [%cal Gc5g5] With tempo, and now the rook really does attack the bishop on g5. } 16. Qe2 Qc8
{ **Verdict:** fine for Black. The queen move does several jobs: it defends g4, it lines up on the
c-file behind the rook and knight so that a sacrifice on b2 is in the air, and it leaves the rook free
to take on g5. Black is a pawn up with the h-file closed, and the standard assessment is that the early
g-pawn advance promises White little. } *

[Line "Soltis Variation: 13.Bh6, 13.Kb1 and 13.g4"]
[Section "9.Bc4: Soltis Variation and 12.Kb1"]
[Summary "White's other 13th moves. Against 13.Bh6 Black exchanges on c4 and g7 and brings a rook to h8; 13.Kb1 leads to the sharpest test of the Soltis; 13.g4 is premature."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. h4 h5
11. O-O-O Rc8 12. Bb3 Ne5 13. Bh6 { The direct attempt to remove the bishop. }
( 13. Kb1 { [%csl Yb1] Useful and flexible, and in practice the most dangerous move: White keeps Bg5, Bh6
and g4 all in hand. } 13... Nc4 14. Bxc4 Rxc4 15. g4 { [%cal Rh4h5] The modern way, sacrificing two pawns
for the files. }
( 15. Nde2 { [%cal Re3h6] The older plan: the knight defends c3 so that Bh6 can follow without allowing
...Rxc3. } 15... b5 16. Bh6 b4 { Black is in time on the queenside and has scored well. } )
15... hxg4 16. h5 Nxh5 17. fxg4 Bxg4 18. Rdg1 Qd7 { Black has two extra pawns and White two open files.
Black's ideas are ...Rfc8 and a sacrifice on c3; White doubles on the g- or h-file. This is the line in
which White has scored best against the Soltis in elite games, and it should be studied before 11...Ne5
is chosen in a game that matters. } )
( 13. g4?! { Premature: White has neither played Kb1 nor pinned the knight. } 13... hxg4 14. h5 Nxh5
15. Bh6 e6 { [%cal Gd8f6,Gd8h4] The precise defence. The queen gets a route to f6 and h4, and the bishop
on b3 is shut out. } 16. Rdg1 Qf6 { White has no way through and is two pawns down. } )
13... Nc4 { The solid answer. Black removes the b3-bishop first and lets the dark-squared bishops come
off on g7, where the king recaptures and clears h8 for a rook. }
( 13... Bxh6 { The classical sacrificial line, and still playable. } 14. Qxh6 Rxc3 15. bxc3 Qa5 { [%cal
Ga5c3,Gf8c8] The queen on h6 is far from the defence and Black has the usual compensation: the wrecked
pawns, the c-file and the knight on e5. It asks more of Black's memory than the main line. } )
14. Bxc4 Rxc4 15. Bxg7 Kxg7 16. g4 { The only way to open lines. }
( 16. Nd5 { Without the dark-squared bishops this exchange holds no terror. } 16... Nxd5 17. exd5 { Black
has no weaknesses and plays ...b5 and ...Qb6 or ...Qa5; the kingside is closed. } )
16... hxg4 17. h5 Rh8 { [%cal Gh8h1] The point of recapturing on g7 with the king: the rook reaches the
h-file in one move and White's attack runs into a defender of equal value. } 18. hxg6 fxg6 19. f4
( 19. e5 { A flashy try that fails. } 19... dxe5 20. Ne6+ Bxe6 21. Qxd8 Rxd8 22. Rxd8 gxf3 { Black has
bishop, knight and a mass of passed pawns for a rook and is winning; he has won every elite game from
here. } )
19... e5 { **Verdict:** good for Black. He strikes in the centre before White can play f5 or e5, and
after 20.Nde2 Nxe4 he has won a second pawn. The position stays sharp, but Black has a healthy plus score
in this line and none of the usual worries about the h-file. } *

[Line "The 12.Kb1 system and the Burnett Variation"]
[Section "9.Bc4: Soltis Variation and 12.Kb1"]
[Summary "The line that drives many Dragon players to the Topalov move order. White makes a useful waiting move, and Black's sharpest answer is a rook sacrifice on c4."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 cxd4 4. Nxd4 Nf6 5. Nc3 g6 6. Be3 Bg7 7. f3 O-O 8. Qd2 Nc6 9. Bc4 Bd7 10. O-O-O
Rc8 11. Bb3 Ne5 12. Kb1 { [%csl Yb1] The modern main line, reckoned the critical test of 11...Ne5. White
makes the one useful move that commits nothing. The king leaves the c-file and covers a2; ...Qa5 no
longer comes with a pin or a check; Nd5 becomes possible because ...Qxd2 is not check; and since h4 has
not been played, the Soltis set-up has nothing to block. } 12... Nc4 { The established move and Jones's
choice for those who play 11...Ne5. The fashionable 12...a6, which Carlsen used against Ivanchuk in
2008, he considers too slow to be best; White's critical reply is 13.h4. }
( 12... Re8 { The most popular of the waiting moves. The rook covers e7, which frees the queen, and it
lets Black answer Bh6 with ...Bh8 and keep the bishop. The main line runs into a version of the Soltis
in which White sacrifices two pawns. } 13. h4 h5 14. g4 hxg4 15. h5 Nxh5 16. Bh6 Kh7 { With great
complications; White has full compensation and Black has to know a lot. } )
13. Bxc4 Rxc4 14. g4 { [%cal Rh2h4,Rh4h5] With the king already safe White can start with the g-pawn.
That rules out ...h5, and h4-h5 will follow. The old verdict was that White is simply better, because
Black's standard counterplay is too slow. } 14... b5 { [%cal Gb5b4] Counterplay has to be immediate. }
15. b3 { [%cal Rb3c4] The critical test. The rook has no good square: 15...Rc8 16.Ndxb5 loses a pawn,
and 15...Rc5 runs into 16.Ne6, when the bishop takes on c5 and the d-file opens against the queen. }
( 15. h4 { Getting on with the attack and leaving the rook alone. } 15... b4 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. exd5 Qa5 {
[%cal Ga5a2,Gc4d4] A race. Black attacks a2 and d5, White plays h5; the position is unclear and both
sides have won games from it. } )
15... b4 { [%cal Gg7c3] The Burnett Variation: Black leaves the rook where it is. It is the c3 sacrifice
in another form, as Jones points out, with the rook given on c4. } 16. bxc4 bxc3 17. Qxc3 Qc7 { [%cal
Gc7c4,Gf8b8] The first target is c4, and the b-file is open for the other rook. } 18. g5 Nh5 {
**Verdict:** Black has given the exchange and a pawn, and has real compensation. The white king has no
pawns in front of it on the b-file, the pawns on a2, c2 and c4 are all weak, and Black's bishops, the
rook on the b-file and the knight heading for f4 are all active while White's attack has not started.
Jones presents this as a complete answer to 12.Kb1, and Black has won most of the few elite games
played. It is the right choice for a player who wants 11...Ne5 as the main system. } *
